FINDING 1 (BLOCKING) — `--ref` + `--opus` mints a certificate over the
officina's own bookkeeping, with a wrong tree hash, silently.
----------------------------------------------------------------------
packages/commands/src/ci.ts forwards `--repo <scratch worktree>` to
runVerify while `--studio` still resolves against the CALLER's cwd. In
verify.ts the exclusion list is built as

    toPosixRelative(repo, studioDir)

i.e. relative to the repo it was handed. On the `--ref` path that repo is
the scratch worktree and the studio is outside it, so the exclusion comes
out as `../../../home/edckt/projects/bisellium/studio` — which matches
nothing in `git ls-tree` of the worktree. The worktree's own `studio/`
subtree is therefore hashed INTO the certificate.

Measured, at 60bc385:

  exclude when --repo is the real repo : ["studio",".bisellium"]
  exclude when --repo is the worktree  : ["../../../home/edckt/projects/bisellium/studio",".bisellium"]

  sourceTreeHash(worktree, ["studio",".bisellium","examples/"], HEAD)
      = 6dd3b180be9702da73bec3c928c5eebd73e65293   <- correct; equals what
        the branch's own tests/lint/types certificates carry, and equals
        sourceTreeHash(real repo, same excludes, 59e7e29)
  sourceTreeHash(worktree, ci --ref's excludes,      HEAD)
      = 0cb99d4d6759dbc388cde1cdd40402b17d9559c9   <- what ci --ref --opus writes

Confirmed end to end by running the exact argument vector runCi builds,
against a throwaway copy of studio/ so the real officina was untouched:

  node --import tsx packages/cli/src/main.ts verify W-031 \
      --repo /tmp/claude-1000/w031r1-2 --allow-dirty --studio $TMPDIR/studiocopy

  probationes: { tests: { ... certifies: tree:0cb99d4d6759dbc388cde1cdd40402b17d9559c9 }, ... }

Consequences:
  - Direct breach of the standing rule "A certificate names the source
    tree, not the officina's own bookkeeping." The hash moves whenever any
    opus front matter, ci log or receipt is committed.
  - The prefix is `tree:`, not `dirty:` — it looks authoritative.
  - It can never match what `verify` or `check` computes for the same
    commit, so the certificate is stale from the instant it is written.
  - Behaviour 5's spec sentence ("records gate certificates identical to
    what verify would record for the same tree") is FALSE on this path.
    The behaviour-5 test only ever exercises the current-tree path; no test
    combines `--ref` with `--opus`, which is the combination the brief's
    Acceptance paragraph describes ("a QA agent reviewing a PR runs one
    command"). Nothing in the code or the usage line forbids it.
  - No warning, no refusal. It writes real officina bookkeeping.

The fix is small — on the `--ref` path the exclusion has to be computed
against the repo whose tree is being hashed, e.g. by pointing verify at the
worktree's own studio, or by passing the studio as a repo-relative path —
but it needs a test that runs `--ref` and `--opus` together and compares
the hash against a plain `verify` of the same commit.

FINDING 2 (must fix, same line of code) — `--allow-dirty` is hardcoded.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
    const verifyArgs = [opusId, "--repo", execRepo, "--allow-dirty"];

A caller of `bisellium ci --opus <id>` on a dirty tree gets `dirty:<hash>`
certificates where `bisellium verify <id>` would have exited 2 and written
nothing. That refusal is the guard adopted after cascade 4b — "a
certificate is a claim about what was actually run" (verify.ts) — and `ci`
removes it with no way to ask for it back. Behaviour 5's equality claim is
false here too, and the behaviour-5 fixture never exercises it (its repo is
committed clean, so `dirty` is false on both sides and the two runs agree
for the wrong reason). On the `--ref` path the flag is not needed at all: a
fresh checkout plus a gitignored node_modules is clean. Either drop it or
surface it as an explicit `bisellium ci --allow-dirty`.

FINDING 3 (advisory) — behaviour 4's red does not demonstrate behaviour 4.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
reds/W-031/03.log and 04.log carry byte-identical assertion details
(`ref=1 workingTree=1`) and are both reproduced exactly by one mutation,
M2 above. The mutation that separates behaviour 4 from behaviour 3 — skip
the install, borrow the parent's node_modules — is M1, and it was never
recorded. Behaviour 4 is this opus's load-bearing claim; its red should be
the one that only it can catch. The test is fine (M1 proves it bites); the
evidence is thin. Related tool gap, not the builder's: `bisellium red`'s
header has no field for the mutation, so no red log in this officina
states what was broken.

FINDING 4 (advisory) — smaller things
--------------------------------------
  a. A step killed by STEP_TIMEOUT_MS (10 min) returns status null ->
     `r.status ?? 1`, and is reported as an ordinary step failure. A
     ten-minute hang and a real red are indistinguishable in the output.
  b. `opts.provider ?? selectProvider()` — every test injects
     gitWorktreeProvider, so the production default is uncovered. treehouse
     is not on PATH here, so `--ref` took the git path throughout. Same
     shape as `bisellium run`, so consistent with the house, but noted.
  c. `bisellium ci` is not in CLAUDE.md's Commands list, docs/, or
     .claude/commands/ on this branch. W-030 exists to make the CLI
     discoverable; a command whose whole purpose is that reviewers stop
     hand-building worktrees should be findable without reading main.ts.
  d. When step 5 blocks, `ci --opus` gives no hint that `bisellium verify
     <id>` is the single-opus route. One line of stderr would save a
     reviewer the deduction.

