The --opus strictness question — CORRECT AS BUILT. Do not soften it.
---------------------------------------------------------------------
First, a correction to the premise: the 10 blocking findings are NOT all
other opera's. `traditio.present opera/W-031.md — active item (building)
has no handoff` is W-031's own, and W-031 is one of the 6 items putting
`wip.cap` over 4. Two of the ten are this opus's.

Second, on the principle. Step 5 is `npm run -s check -- studio --repo .`
because that is the line in ci.yml. Scoping it to one opus would require a
second, opus-aware check path — a second source of truth for what "green"
means, which is exactly what behaviour 7 exists to forbid. And the rules
that fire here are collective by construction: `wip.cap` is a statement
about the officina, not about an opus; `lesson.recurrent` is about the
corpus. "Another opus's missing red is not my problem" is the reasoning
those rules were written to overrule.

Third, the division of labour is already right and already documented by
this opus's own gates: `verify <opus>` runs what the manifest declares for
one opus and mints its certificates; `ci` reproduces the runner, and the
runner has never cared whose finding it is. The escape hatch exists, is
narrower, and is the one W-031 itself used.

Coarse, yes. Correct, also yes. The only change worth making is finding
4(d) — say so in one line of stderr instead of leaving the reviewer to
work it out.
